ifold; can it sit as a dense open subset of a 4-manifold? A dense open subset of a manifold that is itself a manifold of the same dimension and whose complement has empty interior — e.g., remove a Cantor set from a 4-manifold: the complement is open dense; it's a manifold. Yes. So the added points can have empty interior. In such a case, clause (f) as written in F2a ("p in M' minus iota(M)") would be satisfiable by a point of the Cantor set if it's in the future of some q ∈ iota(M), whereas F2b requires an interior point. Does the "proper future extension" notion require "adds points to the future" in a substantive sense? F2b's repair was accepted because clause (f) was escapable. F2a's clause (f) is the pre-repair wording, and the F2a must_not_conflate/e (e) says "Ric=0 classically ... so this is a pointwise statement" — fine.

